      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "To assess the awareness of hypertension and dyslipidemia in a semirural population of young adults."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "A cohort of 5,707 individuals was surveyed by a questionnaire. The awareness analysis was done on 1,454 subjects screened for cardiovascular risk factors 5 years earlier."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Among the respondents (n = 3,699, 65%), the prevalence of hypertension among blacks and whites was 11% and 7.3% (P < 0.0001), respectively; dyslipidemia, 4.7% and 5.8% (P = 0.27). Results from the screening showed a prevalence of 11.4% in blacks versus 6.0% in whites (P < 0.0001) for hypertension; 14.2% versus 17.9% (P = 0.12) for dyslipidemia. Males had a higher prevalence of dyslipidemia than females (24% vs. 12%, P = 0.001). Five years later, among those informed of their hypertension, males were more likely to be aware of their hypertension than females [odds ratio (95% confidence interval) = 5.0 (1.4-17.5)]. Increasing age [1.04 (1.0-1.1)], positive parental history of coronary heart disease [2.6 (1.5-4.3)], and higher education level [2.1 (1.2-33.6)] were associated with the awareness of dyslipidemia. Awareness of the condition increased the proportion of subjects receiving treatment for hypertension, but did not change the proportion receiving treatment for dyslipidemia."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "These results underscore the need for cardiovascular health education efforts in this population group."
        }
      ]},
